Subject: [RFC PATCH v3 0/4] fs: keep the end of a file in EFI Write()

EFI_FILE_PROTOCOL.Write() cuts a FAT file at the end of the written
range, so a write in the middle of a file loses the data after it.
This series keeps that data. It is still an RFC, mainly for the new
interface.

fs_write() does not change. The fatwrite command depends on it
replacing a whole file. The new fs_pwrite() keeps the tail. FAT and
exFAT implement it. Other filesystems, such as ext4, return -ENOSYS,
and EFI Write() then falls back to fs_write().

Changes since v2:

- Rename fs_overwrite() to fs_pwrite() and file_fat_overwrite() to
  file_fat_pwrite(), following pwrite(2).
- fs_pwrite() returns -ENOSYS when the filesystem cannot keep the
  tail, instead of falling back to fs_write(). EFI Write() now falls
  back explicitly.
- A zero-length write with keep_tail no longer frees the clusters.
- Rename fs_write_with() to _fs_write(), with keep_tail as the last
  argument.
- Document the tail behaviour of fs_write() and the return values of
  fs_pwrite().
- Add exFAT support, as a separate patch.
- Add dm tests that write into the middle of a FAT and an exFAT file.
- Add an efi_selftest that overwrites the start of u-boot.txt.
- Fix the EFI commit message: the file is cut at the end of the last
  write (offset + len), and the fix only covers FAT and exFAT.

Possibly future (I would do my best if this series is accepted):

- pwrite for ext4
- truncation in EFI SetInfo()

Cedric Hombourger (4):
  fs: fat: add a write that keeps the end of the file
  fs: add fs_pwrite() to write without trimming the file
  fs: exfat: add a write that keeps the end of the file
  efi_loader: do not trim files in Write()

